From f0154e0000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH 0154e] evergo: dump the interrupted stack in the panic path
A call through a NULL function pointer leaves pc, lr and fp all zero, so
the register dump names nobody. Dump the first 48 words at the interrupted
sp: kernel stacks are wired so the reads cannot fault, and the caller
frame records in there can be symbolized offline against the same binary.
Output goes through panic_plain -> console -> SWEETLOG, so the ring
carries it out even when the screen is dark.
---
diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
index 24f1abc..745fa07 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -502,6 +502,34 @@ panic_with_thread_kernel_state(const char *msg, arm_saved_state_t *ss)
 	    state->x[24], state->x[25], state->x[26], state->x[27],
 	    state->x[28], state->fp, state->lr, state->sp,
 	    state->pc, state->cpsr, state->esr, state->far);
+
+	/*
+	 * evergo (0154e): when the fault is a call through a corrupted pointer,
+	 * pc AND lr AND fp can all be zero (observed: instruction fetch abort at
+	 * 0, esr 0x86000005), and the register dump above then names nobody. The
+	 * interrupted stack still holds the caller frame records, so dump the
+	 * first words at sp. Values inside the kernel text range can be
+	 * symbolized offline with sym_kernel.py against the same kernel binary.
+	 *
+	 * The stack grows down, so [sp, sp+N) walks back into the frames of the
+	 * callers; kernel stacks are wired, so these reads cannot fault.
+	 */
+	{
+		uint64_t ksp = state->sp;
+
+		if (ksp != 0) {
+			panic_plain("SWEET STACK: sp 0x%016llx, dumping 48 words\n",
+			    (unsigned long long)ksp);
+			for (int i = 0; i < 48; i++) {
+				uint64_t word =
+				    *(const volatile uint64_t *)(ksp + (uint64_t)i * 8);
+
+				panic_plain("SWEET STACK %02d @0x%016llx: 0x%016llx\n",
+				    i, (unsigned long long)(ksp + (uint64_t)i * 8),
+				    word);
+			}
+		}
+	}
 }
 
 void
